    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. what about the actual stacking pattern though? like, if we use a column stack it's easier to load but i've heard interlocking patterns are better for preventing things from shifting. is there a specific way to do the interlocking so the weight doesnt get lopsided?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Column stacking is fine if the load is uniform, but if you're worried about shifting, you're right to look at interlocking patterns. A simple, staggered pattern—like bricklaying—is generally the best approach to prevent lateral movement. Imagine you're stacking crates so that the weight of the upper layer rests in the valleys created by the layer below it. This distributes the load more evenly, preventing that lopsided feeling you mentioned. Always ensure that the footprint of the upper layer is completely supported by the lower layer beneath it.",
      "source": "target",
