    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah that bricklaying thing sounds good. my only concern is the high-density aspect. when we have so many pallets packed together in tight spaces, sometimes things just seem to... move or get knocked slightly out of alignment during the shifting process. how do we stop minor imbalances from turning into a bigger problem during the shift?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When you're packing pallets tightly in high-density racking, the risk of minor shifts escalating is real, and it's almost as much of a structural issue as a loading issue. It's very important to use appropriate dunnage or bracing between the stacks if the load isn't fully contained by the pallet structure itself. If the load is shifting, it means the friction between the pallets isn't enough to counteract the forces during movement. For the best results, secure the load *to* the pallet, not just *on* it.",
      "source": "target",
